-- Actor authority - the smallest worked example consulting the
-- transition's actor (v0).
--
-- Domain: a business approves documents (invoices, contracts, change
-- orders). Some people are authorised to approve which kinds of
-- documents; some are not. Authority is granted explicitly and may
-- later be revoked. The historical record of which approvals
-- happened, by which actor, under which authority - survives every
-- subsequent change.
--
-- The forcing question: *who proposed this transition?* The actor
-- is not a domain payload, so it should not be a transformation
-- parameter. It is *transition context* - carried on the Transition
-- value object alongside the args, and reachable from inside a
-- transformation body (require, assert, emit, let, for) through the
-- reserved term `$actor` (`Term::Actor` in the IR).
--
-- `$actor` is *not* reachable from invariant bodies. Invariants
-- evaluate against admitted state with no transition in scope; an
-- invariant that reaches for `$actor` raises `EvalError::UnboundActor`
-- at evaluation time. This is the require-vs-invariant doctrine
-- (from Example 3) made enforceable by the runtime, not convention:
-- authority checks live in `require`.
--
-- No invariants. Authority is a precondition at admission time, not
-- a continuous property of admitted state. Tying Approval to live
-- MayApprove via an invariant would either reject every revocation
-- (because historical approvals now break the rule) or cascade-retract
-- the historical approvals themselves. Neither matches the real-
-- world semantics. The require-vs-invariant lesson from Example 3
-- is the same lesson here.


transformation grant_approval_authority(actor, doc_type):
    assert MayApprove(actor, doc_type)
    emit ApprovalAuthorityGranted(actor, doc_type)


transformation revoke_approval_authority(actor, doc_type):
    require MayApprove(actor, doc_type)
    retract MayApprove(actor, doc_type)
    emit ApprovalAuthorityRevoked(actor, doc_type)


-- The transformation declares no `actor` parameter. The actor flows
-- through transition context and is read via $actor.
--
-- require $actor consults the proposing actor at admission time.
-- assert Approval(..., $actor) stamps the proposing actor onto the
-- durable record so the audit log answers "who approved this?".
transformation approve_document(doc_id, doc_type):
    require MayApprove($actor, doc_type)
    assert Approval(doc_id, doc_type, $actor)
    emit DocumentApproved(doc_id, $actor)
